  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nUpper level heights on the rebound as long-wave ridging builds into \nthe northern Great Lakes early this afternoon. Overhead ridge \nbuilding part of a maturing long-wave amplification process...driven \nin large part by deep troughing building into the western NOAM. \nThose building heights and attendant deep layer subsidence resulting \nin mostly sunny skies today, with that sunshine helping temperatures \nreturn to near or a bit above specific normal values for today's \ndate (current readings in the 50s and 60s). \n\nUpper level ridging will continue to mature as it centers itself \nacross the area this weekend. Current Gulf of America Hurricane \nIsaias will continue to pivot rapidly north, eventually cutting up \ninto the Tennessee and Ohio River Valleys later this weekend. Ridge \ndoes begin to break down heading through next week as various pieces \nof energy out of upstream troughing eject east across central NOAM. \n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nTemperature trends and addressing any shower potential through the \nperiod.\n\nDetails: \n\nBiggest story for this weekend will be the well above normal \ntemperatures...fueled by those building overhead heights and \nintensifying return flow on the backside of departing surface high. \nTop-down thermal profiles easily support highs both Saturday and \nSunday up into the 70s for most, with perhaps some areas of \nnorthwest lower Michigan making a run at 80 on Saturday when south \nwind produced downslope warming will be most notable. Conditions \nshould largely remain dry, although will need to watch the northern \nextent of deeper moisture tied to decaying Isaias late Saturday \nnight/early Sunday as it pivots northeast through the lower Ohio \nValley (although current trends support any rains remaining just to \nour southeast).\n\nStart of next work week again looks mild, although exact \ntemperatures details will be dictated by timing of slow southeast \ndiving cold front. Looks like areas south of the big bridge remain \nin the warm sector much of the day, supporting highs back up into \nthe 70s. Perhaps a bit cooler across eastern upper owning to the \npassing front. Still, easily looking at well above normal \ntemperatures up into the 60s even up there. Front will slowly work \nsouth across the area Monday night. While the vast majority of \ndeeper mid level support becomes detached to our north, probably \nstill looking at the threat for a few showers along and just behind \nthe front later Monday into early Tuesday.\n\nTemperatures definitely look to trend to more normal-like levels for \nthe rest of next week. Weather does look to potentially become a \ntouch more active the second half of the week as core of western \ntroughing rotates northeast into the central Plains and northern \nConus. Plume of deeper subtropical moisture will likely accompany \nthis troughing and attendant maturing cold front...driving good \npotential for some much needed rains. Timing subject to change of \ncourse, but definitely looking at some wet weather potential at \ntimes Wednesday night through Friday.",
